MERRITT: What's it say?

262
00:13:20,742 --> 00:13:22,284
"Now you don't. "

263
00:13:23,119 --> 00:13:26,413
"A rose by any other name... "

264
00:13:30,168 --> 00:13:31,251
(DANIEL LAUGHS)

265
00:13:31,336 --> 00:13:33,003
JACK: Guys, what's happening?

266
00:13:33,671 --> 00:13:34,671
Whoa.

267
00:13:35,256 --> 00:13:36,507
Look at that.

268
00:13:37,175 --> 00:13:38,175
Whoa.

269
00:13:43,515 --> 00:13:44,640
JACK: It's gas!

270
00:13:44,849 --> 00:13:46,934
MERRITT: Relax. Just dry ice.

271
00:13:47,727 --> 00:13:48,727
Cool.

272
00:13:49,687 --> 00:13:51,063
Wait. What do you think this is all about?

273
00:13:51,272 --> 00:13:52,773
Hang on. Hang on.

274
00:13:56,444 --> 00:13:57,611
(MOANS)

275
00:13:57,862 --> 00:13:58,987
I got nothin'.

276
00:13:59,197 --> 00:14:00,906
Okay. Thank you. Thank you for the delay.

277
00:14:01,116 --> 00:14:02,991
I'm just trying to create the space for wisdom.

278
00:14:03,201 --> 00:14:06,203
Okay, so you're like Buddha,
if he wasn't so enlightened.

279
00:14:06,287 --> 00:14:07,663
And you're like Jesus,

280
00:14:07,747 --> 00:14:10,290
if he was arrogant
and all of his miracles were fake.

281
00:14:10,375 --> 00:14:13,627
Okay, lovebirds, get a room.
Danny, be honest. Did you do this?

282
00:14:13,711 --> 00:14:14,837
No. Wait, did you?

283
00:14:14,921 --> 00:14:16,213
I wish.

284
00:14:16,714 --> 00:14:18,966
Why didn't anyone ask me if I did it?

285
00:14:19,968 --> 00:14:20,968
Oh, great.

286
00:14:26,573 --> 00:14:28,074
JACK: Electricity is out.

287
00:14:29,243 --> 00:14:30,743
Well, let's check.

288
00:14:34,999 --> 00:14:36,040
Whoa.

289
00:14:44,591 --> 00:14:46,175
(HENLEY LAUGHS)

290
00:14:47,761 --> 00:14:49,262
HENLEY: Blueprints.

291
00:14:50,097 --> 00:14:51,431
DANIEL: They're incredible.

292
00:14:52,891 --> 00:14:54,267
JACK: Who do you think did this?

293
00:14:54,351 --> 00:14:56,519
I don't know, but I really want to meet them.

294
00:14:58,522 --> 00:15:00,106
It's a show.

295
00:15:02,568 --> 00:15:03,735
Wow.

296
00:15:19,960 --> 00:15:21,377
ANNOUNCER: Merritt McKinney,

297
00:15:23,130 --> 00:15:24,422
Daniel Atlas,

298
00:15:25,799 --> 00:15:27,050
Henley Reeves,

299
00:15:29,053 --> 00:15:30,762
Jack Wilder.

300
00:15:31,555 --> 00:15:35,475
Arthur Tressler and the MGM Grand
proudly presents

301
00:15:38,228 --> 00:15:40,313
The Four Horsemen.

302
00:15:40,647 --> 00:15:41,731
(AUDIENCE CHEERING)

303
00:15:41,815 --> 00:15:42,982
MERRITT: Thank you.

304
00:15:43,275 --> 00:15:45,485
Tonight we would like to try something

305
00:15:45,569 --> 00:15:47,987
that will, well, set us a bit apart.

306
00:15:48,155 --> 00:15:51,741
For our final trick,
we're gonna do something never before

307
00:15:51,825 --> 00:15:53,826
seen on a Las Vegas stage.

308
00:15:54,828 --> 00:15:57,163
Or any stage for that matter.

309
00:15:57,998 --> 00:15:59,457
Ladies and gentlemen,

310
00:16:01,752 --> 00:16:02,835
tonight

311
00:16:03,754 --> 00:16:05,505
we are going to rob a bank.

312
00:16:05,631 --> 00:16:07,340
(AUDIENCE CHEERING LOUDLY)

313
00:16:16,517 --> 00:16:18,601
That's a lot of excitement for a crime.

314
00:16:19,353 --> 00:16:23,022
HENLEY: I'm getting excited.
What about you, people?

315
00:16:23,107 --> 00:16:24,607
(CHEERING INTENSIFIES)

316
00:16:28,487 --> 00:16:30,696
BOTH: One, two, three!

317
00:16:39,414 --> 00:16:42,667
Sorry, sir. There's absolutely no recording
allowed during the performance.

318
00:16:42,751 --> 00:16:44,335
Oh, yeah, yeah, sure, of course.

319
00:16:44,419 --> 00:16:45,753
- (BEEPS)
- Let me just put this away.

320
00:16:46,004 --> 00:16:47,213
Actually, I need to take that.

321
00:16:47,381 --> 00:16:49,173
After the show, I can come
and give it back to you.

322
00:16:50,551 --> 00:16:51,551
Okay.

323
00:16:53,095 --> 00:16:54,387
(CHUCKLING)

324
00:16:55,055 --> 00:16:56,764
DANIEL: Okay, okay.

325
00:16:57,015 --> 00:16:58,891
Now, please, please, settle down.

326
00:16:59,601 --> 00:17:02,436
Now who here has a bank
they would like us to rob?

327
00:17:03,689 --> 00:17:05,690
That's a lot of people with a vendetta.

328
00:17:05,858 --> 00:17:07,567
So we'll choose one at random then.

329
00:17:07,693 --> 00:17:08,943
(CLAMORING)

330
00:17:09,236 --> 00:17:11,696
My associates will make sure
it's random. Right?

331
00:17:12,698 --> 00:17:13,739
Thank you, Merritt.

332
00:17:13,824 --> 00:17:15,616
We are looking at row number 5.

333
00:17:15,701 --> 00:17:16,701
Where is that?

334
00:17:16,910 --> 00:17:19,745
And Henley, could I please
have a random seat number?

335
00:17:20,706 --> 00:17:22,206
Oh. Lucky number 13.

336
00:17:22,416 --> 00:17:24,041
- B-5-13. Where are you?
- (AUDIENCE APPLAUDING)

337
00:17:24,251 --> 00:17:27,879
Sir, please, stand up. Ah, there you are. Hi.

338
00:17:29,506 --> 00:17:33,301
Could you just confirm for me that this
is, in fact, your seat? B-5-13.

339
00:17:33,385 --> 00:17:34,343
Yes.

340
00:17:34,428 --> 00:17:36,429
Okay. Wonderful.
Now, could you please tell us

341
00:17:36,555 --> 00:17:37,889
your name and the name of your bank?

342
00:17:38,056 --> 00:17:40,266
Well, my name is Etienne Forcier.

343
00:17:40,517 --> 00:17:43,895
And my bank, it's Credit Republicain de Paris.

344
00:17:44,062 --> 00:17:45,229
French. Okay. Uh...

345
00:17:45,439 --> 00:17:46,898
We were hoping for something
a little more local,

346
00:17:47,065 --> 00:17:49,317
a kind of mom-and-pop
credit union with no security.

347
00:17:49,401 --> 00:17:51,360
But that's fine. A promise is a promise.

348
00:17:51,570 --> 00:17:52,737
Could you please come up to the stage

349
00:17:52,821 --> 00:17:54,155
- and we'll rob your bank.
- (CHEERING)

350
00:17:54,239 --> 00:17:56,115
And while he does that...

351
00:17:56,408 --> 00:17:59,535
There is someone here tonight
without whom we would just be

352
00:17:59,620 --> 00:18:02,747
four magicians working the circuit,
trying to get...

353
00:18:02,915 --> 00:18:04,332
Well, actually, trying to get here.

354
00:18:04,416 --> 00:18:07,335
You probably know this man,
if not from one of the many, many

355
00:18:07,419 --> 00:18:08,502
companies he puts his name on.

356
00:18:08,587 --> 00:18:12,215
He is our friend. He is our benefactor.
Mr. Arthur Tressler.

357
00:18:12,299 --> 00:18:14,258
Please, stand up, Art. Please, stand up.

358
00:18:14,468 --> 00:18:17,261
MERRITT: The only man here with
the Queen's cell phone number.

359
00:18:18,222 --> 00:18:20,223
Actually, please, stay standing, Art.

360
00:18:21,266 --> 00:18:23,392
I want to say that when we came
to Mr. Tressler,

361
00:18:23,602 --> 00:18:27,188
we promised that, as a unit, we could become
the biggest name in magic.

362
00:18:27,272 --> 00:18:28,814
HENLEY: So we wanted to say, "Thank you. "

363
00:18:28,899 --> 00:18:30,733
And by the way, Art,
you notice on the sign out front,

364
00:18:30,817 --> 00:18:32,735
we made sure we put your name on top.

365
00:18:33,111 --> 00:18:37,198
If you turn out to be as good
as you think you are, dear girl,

366
00:18:37,282 --> 00:18:39,325
that won't be necessary much longer.

367
00:18:39,576 --> 00:18:40,826
HENLEY: We haven't done our closer yet.

368
00:18:41,078 --> 00:18:42,078
- (GRUNTS)
- Why don't you watch it

369
00:18:42,162 --> 00:18:43,371
and then you can decide for yourself.

370
00:18:43,455 --> 00:18:45,706
Ladies and gentlemen, Arthur Tressler!

371
00:18:45,791 --> 00:18:47,458
DANIEL: Thank you.
And, of course, once again,

372
00:18:47,542 --> 00:18:49,502
the Cardinal of Clairvoyance,
Merritt McKinney.

373
00:18:50,003 --> 00:18:53,214
Etienne, what Jack
is bringing to the stage now,

374
00:18:53,298 --> 00:18:56,926
is what we in the magic world
call a teleportation helmet.

375
00:18:57,135 --> 00:19:03,182
You will need to wear this, as it will allow you
to literally fold through space and time

376
00:19:03,433 --> 00:19:05,601
to your bank in the...

377
00:19:05,811 --> 00:19:06,811
8th?

378
00:19:07,020 --> 00:19:08,938
-9th arrondissement.
- Yeah.

379
00:19:09,147 --> 00:19:13,109
Now, once you are there, we will be able
to speak with you through this helmet.

380
00:19:13,193 --> 00:19:14,944
- Now if... Ah!
- Ow.

381
00:19:15,153 --> 00:19:16,821
- Oh, my God, that's beautiful.
- (AUDIENCE CHUCKLING)

382
00:19:16,989 --> 00:19:19,782
It has the added attraction of being
very stylish.

383
00:19:19,866 --> 00:19:23,327
It's about time the French learned
from America on that subject.

384
00:19:24,663 --> 00:19:25,788
Is that a beautiful piece of headgear?

385
00:19:25,872 --> 00:19:27,498
- Thank you. Thank you...
- It is.

386
00:19:28,542 --> 00:19:31,127
Thank you. Thank you very much.

387
00:19:31,211 --> 00:19:32,420
MERRITT: Mais oui. Mais oui.

388
00:19:32,504 --> 00:19:33,838
But before you go anywhere,

389
00:19:33,964 --> 00:19:36,090
could you please, pick a card, any card.

390
00:19:36,174 --> 00:19:37,174
Not that card. Oh!

391
00:19:37,509 --> 00:19:40,928
No, that's an old American joke.
You can take that one.

392
00:19:41,013 --> 00:19:42,471
Okay. This one?

393
00:19:42,681 --> 00:19:44,640
Show it to your friends in Section B.
But not to us.

394
00:19:46,476 --> 00:19:48,477
Okay, great.
Now if you could just sign your name there.

395
00:19:48,562 --> 00:19:50,980
- Yes.
- In English, if possible.

396
00:19:51,356 --> 00:19:52,732
- MERRITT: That's good.
- Thanks.

397
00:19:53,859 --> 00:19:54,984
Put it in your pocket.

398
00:19:55,360 --> 00:19:57,737
And now for one tiny detail.

399
00:19:59,197 --> 00:20:01,324
(AUDIENCE GASPING)

400
00:20:06,038 --> 00:20:07,580
(AUDIENCE CHEERING)

401
00:20:15,172 --> 00:20:19,717
Now, Etienne, let's step into
this cockamamie contraption.

402
00:20:19,926 --> 00:20:21,844
And I'll step off of it. Bonne chance.

403
00:20:22,262 --> 00:20:25,139
It's 11:50 p. m. here in Vegas.

404
00:20:25,223 --> 00:20:29,894
That's 8:50 a. m. in Paris.
Your bank opens in less than 10 minutes.

405
00:20:30,103 --> 00:20:31,145
One,

406
00:20:31,229 --> 00:20:32,271
two...

407
00:20:32,356 --> 00:20:33,397
Three.

408
00:20:35,233 --> 00:20:36,275
(ALL GASPING)

409
00:20:36,693 --> 00:20:37,902
MERRITT: What the fuck?

410
00:20:38,070 --> 00:20:39,695
- Wow! Etienne?
- Whoa. Etienne?

411
00:20:39,905 --> 00:20:41,739
It wasn't supposed to happen
like that, was it?

412
00:20:41,907 --> 00:20:42,948
HENLEY: Etienne?

413
00:20:43,575 --> 00:20:45,576
I liked that little French guy.

414
00:20:45,744 --> 00:20:46,786
Where did he go?

415
00:20:46,912 --> 00:20:48,412
(CARS HONKING)

416
00:20:55,754 --> 00:20:57,296
Wait, there he is.

417
00:20:59,591 --> 00:21:01,634
No, no, no, no, please, please, please.

418
00:21:01,885 --> 00:21:03,761
This is Daniel Atlas. Can you hear me?

419
00:21:04,638 --> 00:21:07,056
- Etienne? Are you okay?
- Yes.

420
00:21:07,140 --> 00:21:09,517
Perfect. What do you see in there?

421
00:21:11,478 --> 00:21:12,603
Money.

422
00:21:13,105 --> 00:21:14,105
Is this real?

423
00:21:14,272 --> 00:21:16,774
Yes. Looks like three million or so
Euros' worth.

424
00:21:17,484 --> 00:21:18,776
(SPEAKS FRENCH)

425
00:21:18,985 --> 00:21:20,736
Okay, now, here's what
we're gonna need you to do.

426
00:21:20,821 --> 00:21:24,323
I want you to take the card that you signed
out of your pocket.

427
00:21:24,449 --> 00:21:26,617
And I want you to take the ticket stub
from tonight's show

428
00:21:26,827 --> 00:21:28,994
and I want you to put it right there
in the middle of the money.

429
00:21:33,125 --> 00:21:34,291
Now drop it.

430
00:21:46,304 --> 00:21:49,432
Now, on the side of your helmet
you should feel a button.

431
00:21:49,641 --> 00:21:50,808
Don't press it just yet.

432
00:21:51,017 --> 00:21:55,604
That button activates an air duct
that connects Paris to Las Vegas.

433
00:21:55,814 --> 00:21:57,148
Okay, good. Now you can press it.

434
00:21:57,315 --> 00:22:00,860
All right, now, Etienne, hold on tight.
You might feel a bit of a vacuum.

435
00:22:00,944 --> 00:22:01,944
(CHUCKLES)

436
00:22:03,196 --> 00:22:04,864
(AIR WHOOSHING)

437
00:22:09,536 --> 00:22:10,661
(WHOOPS)

438
00:22:18,837 --> 00:22:20,463
Whoo! (LAUGHING)

439
00:22:32,017 --> 00:22:33,350
Oh. Wait a second.

440
00:22:33,560 --> 00:22:34,977
- Huh.
- (RUMBLING)

441
00:22:46,156 --> 00:22:47,656
WOMAN: Oh, merde.

442
00:22:48,074 --> 00:22:49,700
(CHEERING)

443
00:23:07,344 --> 00:23:08,510
(CHUCKLES)

444
00:23:12,515 --> 00:23:13,724
(LAUGHING)

445
00:23:27,072 --> 00:23:28,906
Thank you, Etienne. Thank you, everyone!

446
00:23:29,407 --> 00:23:30,532
We are The Four Horsemen.

447
00:23:30,742 --> 00:23:33,035
- And good night!
- Good night!

448
00:23:41,586 --> 00:23:43,045
(CELL PHONE RINGING)

449
00:23:45,840 --> 00:23:47,049
Hold up here.

450
00:23:47,425 --> 00:23:48,717
Dylan Rhodes.

451
00:23:50,512 --> 00:23:52,513
What? I don't think I heard you correctly.

452
00:23:52,722 --> 00:23:54,056
Did you say magicians?

453
00:23:54,266 --> 00:23:55,891
Yeah, Dylan, magicians.

454
00:23:56,268 --> 00:23:57,351
Where are you now?

455
00:23:57,560 --> 00:23:59,436
I'm at Aria.
I'm heading up there to grab 'em now.

456
00:24:03,775 --> 00:24:05,567
FBl! Hands where I can see them.

457
00:24:05,777 --> 00:24:07,069
Let's go. Get 'em up. Come on.

458
00:24:07,237 --> 00:24:08,237
Uno minuto.

459
00:24:08,530 --> 00:24:10,239
FULLER: Let's go. Get 'em up.

460
00:24:10,448 --> 00:24:11,740
Put the book down.

461
00:24:11,950 --> 00:24:13,575
Okay. You got me.

462
00:24:13,785 --> 00:24:15,202
- Freeze! Hands in the air!
- HENLEY: (GASPS) Oh, my!

463
00:24:15,787 --> 00:24:18,539
Do one of you guys
mind giving us a hand with our bags?

464
00:24:19,082 --> 00:24:20,082
Let's go.

465
00:24:25,755 --> 00:24:27,381
(PEOPLE APPLAUDING)

466
00:24:37,934 --> 00:24:39,768
DYLAN: Boss, please tell me this is a joke.

467
00:24:39,978 --> 00:24:42,896
I just got Willy Mears
to finger Paulie Attanasio.

468
00:24:43,106 --> 00:24:46,358
I'm a month, two tops,
away from blowing this whole thing open.

469
00:24:46,443 --> 00:24:48,610
- Get Turkelson.
- He's in Atlantic City.

470
00:24:48,778 --> 00:24:51,488
What about Cowan? Look at him.
He's just sitting there on his ass.

471
00:24:51,614 --> 00:24:52,823
Hilarious, Rhodes.

472
00:24:53,283 --> 00:24:54,408
I love you.

473
00:24:54,743 --> 00:24:55,784
Asshole.

474
00:24:55,994 --> 00:24:57,411
I don't have time for this magic crap.

475
00:24:57,620 --> 00:25:00,164
This crap just pulled
three million Euro out of a Parisian bank.

476
00:25:00,248 --> 00:25:01,332
- That's how much they got?
- Yeah.

477
00:25:01,499 --> 00:25:03,000
Actually, 3.2.

478
00:25:07,005 --> 00:25:08,380
- Who is she?
- (MUMBLES)

479
00:25:08,673 --> 00:25:09,673
Who are you?

480
00:25:09,758 --> 00:25:12,926
Alma Dray from Interpol.
I'll be working the investigation with you.

481
00:25:13,136 --> 00:25:14,178
You gotta be kidding me.

482
00:25:14,387 --> 00:25:17,097
It's bad enough they got me chasing down
David Copperfield and Friends.

483
00:25:17,307 --> 00:25:20,392
Now I'm being saddled with,
no offense, with Interpol?

484
00:25:20,602 --> 00:25:22,686
I look forward to working
with you, too, Agent Rhodes.

485
00:25:22,896 --> 00:25:25,773
I'm sorry. I don't think I'm gonna
need your help on this. Okay?

486
00:25:25,982 --> 00:25:27,983
- Where's the French guy?
- I already spoke to him.

487
00:25:28,610 --> 00:25:30,986
Oh, you did?
You spoke to my witness before me?

488
00:25:31,196 --> 00:25:34,198
Your witness?
I thought you didn't want the case.

489
00:25:34,407 --> 00:25:35,991
Besides, he's useless.

490
00:25:36,201 --> 00:25:37,743
Oh, really? Why is that?

491
00:25:37,952 --> 00:25:40,120
Because he believes their magic was real.

492
00:25:40,330 --> 00:25:43,374
Oh, he believes the magic was real?
You're right, he probably is useless.

493
00:25:43,583 --> 00:25:46,502
Is it okay with Interpol if we talk to him, too?

494
00:25:46,586 --> 00:25:48,170
- Mm-hmm.
- Thank you.

495
00:25:49,214 --> 00:25:52,299
I have never stolen a thing in my life, okay?

496
00:25:52,509 --> 00:25:54,718
I tried to give the money back,
but they won't take it.

497
00:25:54,969 --> 00:25:57,429
Okay, okay, okay,
let's just say you robbed a bank.

498
00:25:57,514 --> 00:25:58,680
I did.

499
00:25:58,848 --> 00:26:00,057
Fine. Fantastic. You did.

500
00:26:00,183 --> 00:26:02,726
But since I'm new to this,
can you explain to me how you went

501
00:26:02,811 --> 00:26:05,229
from Las Vegas to Paris in three seconds?

502
00:26:05,647 --> 00:26:07,773
With the teleportation helmet.

503
00:26:08,024 --> 00:26:09,441
Okay, what the hell is going on here?

504
00:26:09,567 --> 00:26:11,235
He was hypnotized during the show.

505
00:26:11,694 --> 00:26:13,404
I told you, he's useless.

506
00:26:13,488 --> 00:26:16,281
Okay, I'll deal with you when I'm done
with this other bullshit.

507
00:26:17,242 --> 00:26:18,867
(CLASSICAL MUSIC PLAYING)

508
00:26:19,702 --> 00:26:20,786
Hey!

509
00:26:20,870 --> 00:26:22,663
FULLER: During the show,
half the audience was hypnotized

510
00:26:22,747 --> 00:26:24,164
to believe they were in the Philharmonic.

511
00:26:24,374 --> 00:26:26,667
I guess that "bullshit" was the trigger word.

512
00:26:26,751 --> 00:26:29,878
Okay. Hey, hey. Écoutez.
Etienne, come on down from there.

513
00:26:29,963 --> 00:26:31,046
You're not in the Philharmonic.

514
00:26:31,339 --> 00:26:33,757
- How do you say "stop" in French?
- Stop.

515
00:26:33,967 --> 00:26:34,967
Tell him to stop.

516
00:26:35,176 --> 00:26:39,346
No, I can't.
You just have to let him finish his movement.

517
00:26:39,556 --> 00:26:41,306
Okay, I need a time-out.

518
00:26:41,433 --> 00:26:42,933
Too many French people in one room.

519
00:26:43,059 --> 00:26:44,560
- Agent Rhodes!
- Huh?

520
00:26:44,644 --> 00:26:47,229
I'm ordered by my bosses to provide a report.

521
00:26:47,439 --> 00:26:50,399
And until we have one, I'm here, like it or not.

522
00:26:50,567 --> 00:26:52,276
So we can work together,

523
00:26:52,402 --> 00:26:56,530
or you can continue to follow behind
asking the same exact questions I'm asking.

524
00:26:56,614 --> 00:26:57,781
It's up to you.

525
00:26:58,074 --> 00:26:59,408
(SNORING)

526
00:27:00,452 --> 00:27:01,493
Hmm.

527
00:27:01,578 --> 00:27:02,786
I'm not finished.

528
00:27:03,037 --> 00:27:06,748
I just flew 12 hours,
after what was already a long day.

529
00:27:06,916 --> 00:27:08,917
And I do not handle jet lag well.

530
00:27:12,380 --> 00:27:14,756
So if you want to see who can be grumpier,

531
00:27:14,883 --> 00:27:16,967
- I promise you, you will lose.
- Hmm.

532
00:27:17,927 --> 00:27:22,181
Mm-hmm. Fine. So which one of these idiots
do you want to talk to first?

533
00:27:22,265 --> 00:27:23,307
Him?

534
00:27:28,938 --> 00:27:30,022
Dylan, for the record,

535
00:27:30,148 --> 00:27:32,316
mentalism has never actually
been proven to be accurate.

536
00:27:32,400 --> 00:27:33,400
Okay.

537
00:27:33,485 --> 00:27:36,278
That is to say, that it's not a science.
It's more for entertainment.

538
00:27:36,362 --> 00:27:37,362
All right.

539
00:27:37,447 --> 00:27:40,824
Just don't believe everything the guy says.
That's all.

540
00:27:42,035 --> 00:27:46,622
Please convey my deepest apologies
to your colleague out there.

541
00:27:46,789 --> 00:27:49,166
I'm really sorry about this
whole Tranny Tuesday thing.

542
00:27:49,292 --> 00:27:50,334
I was out of line.

543
00:27:50,543 --> 00:27:51,668
Tranny Tuesday?

544
00:27:51,836 --> 00:27:53,545
Well, it's an arrangement
he and his wife have.

545
00:27:53,630 --> 00:27:55,380
Or might not have, if you believe

546
00:27:55,465 --> 00:27:57,799
everything Agent Fuller
is saying to be correct.

547
00:27:57,967 --> 00:28:02,554
But isn't there a proud tradition in the FBl
of men wearing dresses?

548
00:28:03,306 --> 00:28:06,975
No shame, Agent Fuller. No shame.

549
00:28:08,811 --> 00:28:11,563
(LAUGHS) I'm just having fun.

550
00:28:11,689 --> 00:28:14,024
Was this your card? No.

551
00:28:14,108 --> 00:28:15,400
See, I knew you weren't
a queen of hearts lady,

552
00:28:15,485 --> 00:28:16,485
and I respect that.

553
00:28:16,569 --> 00:28:18,445
The trick usually works better
when I'm not strapped in here,

554
00:28:18,530 --> 00:28:19,571
but I understand protocol.

555
00:28:19,656 --> 00:28:20,906
Okay, okay.

556
00:28:21,115 --> 00:28:23,700
So, if you had nothing to do with it,

557
00:28:23,785 --> 00:28:26,203
then how did
the playing card get into the vault?

558
00:28:26,454 --> 00:28:27,454
Oh, yes. That would be...

559
00:28:27,539 --> 00:28:29,039
What do the kids call it these days?

560
00:28:29,165 --> 00:28:30,290
Oh, yes, that's right. Magic.

561
00:28:30,375 --> 00:28:32,209
Just answer the question, okay, smartass.

562
00:28:32,460 --> 00:28:35,504
All right. Sorry, sorry, sorry.

563
00:28:36,047 --> 00:28:38,006
You can keep that. Don't share it with him.

564
00:28:38,174 --> 00:28:41,176
It says here you are a mentalist.

565
00:28:41,386 --> 00:28:44,346
- Hmm.
- What exactly is mentalism?

566
00:28:44,556 --> 00:28:47,516
Tricks, mostly. Um, some science.

567
00:28:47,684 --> 00:28:51,520
Targeted guessing
might be a most apt description.

568
00:28:51,729 --> 00:28:54,982
Along with some intuition
and the occasional...

569
00:28:56,651 --> 00:28:58,318
Voices in my head.

570
00:28:58,486 --> 00:28:59,444
(LAUGHS)

571
00:28:59,529 --> 00:29:01,238
Says here you were fairly famous at one point.

572
00:29:01,531 --> 00:29:02,531
Mm-hmm.

573
00:29:02,657 --> 00:29:04,533
You can
even hypnotize people over the phone.

574
00:29:04,701 --> 00:29:07,244
Some big tours
around the U.S. for a few years,

575
00:29:07,328 --> 00:29:09,329
- two TV specials.
- Glory days.

576
00:29:09,414 --> 00:29:12,874
Then your brother/manager disappeared
with all your hard-earned money.

577
00:29:13,084 --> 00:29:14,334
(CLEARS THROAT)
Did your research, did you?

578
00:29:14,502 --> 00:29:16,211
DYLAN: IRS audit. Back taxes.

579
00:29:16,421 --> 00:29:20,549
You've had a long hard slog back
into the limelight, haven't you, Mr. McKinney?

580
00:29:20,758 --> 00:29:25,429
Yes, indeed, it has. And I do appreciate
that trip down Memory Lane.

581
00:29:25,680 --> 00:29:27,639
As far as I understand it,
when the man from Paris

582
00:29:27,724 --> 00:29:28,849
put on the magical helmet...

583
00:29:28,933 --> 00:29:29,933
Listen to me.

584
00:29:30,059 --> 00:29:32,519
If you didn't rob that bank,
then you knew about it.

585
00:29:32,729 --> 00:29:33,812
Which makes you an accomplice.

586
00:29:33,896 --> 00:29:36,315
So if you wanna walk out of here today,
I'd suggest you start...

587
00:29:36,399 --> 00:29:38,984
Now you listen to me, unless you think
there's a D.A. in the state of Nevada

588
00:29:39,068 --> 00:29:40,402
who'd be willing
to make sense of this to a jury,

589
00:29:40,570 --> 00:29:41,737
then we have a show to perform.

590
00:29:41,946 --> 00:29:44,406
And you, Agent Rhodes,
have a drawing board to get back to.

591
00:29:44,532 --> 00:29:45,949
Is this your first date?

592
00:29:47,076 --> 00:29:48,076
What?

593
00:29:48,244 --> 00:29:52,039
No, I mean, obviously, you guys don't
know each other well, if at all.

594
00:29:52,123 --> 00:29:55,667
But, like,
there is a palpable tension in this room.

595
00:29:55,752 --> 00:29:57,419
And before you get involved,

596
00:29:57,629 --> 00:30:01,089
you should consider the fact
that she has a lot of big secrets.

597
00:30:01,257 --> 00:30:06,136
And I know the first one is that
this is your first time off the desk. Isn't it?

598
00:30:06,596 --> 00:30:08,013
You should have said something to him.

599
00:30:08,097 --> 00:30:10,140
This is a weird way for you to find out.

600
00:30:10,391 --> 00:30:12,225
You are literally begging to be arrested.
You know that?

601
00:30:12,310 --> 00:30:14,645
If it means you would actually do it,
then, yeah. But you won't.

602
00:30:14,771 --> 00:30:18,357
Because if you did, it means that you,
and the FBl, and your friends at Interpol,

603
00:30:18,441 --> 00:30:21,943
actually believe,
at an institutional level, in magic.

604
00:30:22,987 --> 00:30:24,613
The press would have a field day.

605
00:30:24,822 --> 00:30:26,490
And we'd be even more famous
than we already are.

606
00:30:26,741 --> 00:30:29,242
And you guys would look like idiots
even more then you already are.

607
00:30:29,452 --> 00:30:31,286
Well, no, not you. But him. Right?

608
00:30:32,288 --> 00:30:34,623
You have,
what we in the business, like to call,

609
00:30:34,707 --> 00:30:36,124
"nothing up your sleeve. "

610
00:30:36,292 --> 00:30:37,292
And you know it.

611
00:30:37,460 --> 00:30:43,131
Don't draw him in and then dump him
because abandonment is a big "area" for him.

612
00:30:43,633 --> 00:30:44,633
Mommy?

613
00:30:45,468 --> 00:30:46,718
Daddy.

614
00:30:47,053 --> 00:30:48,261
Ah! You have big daddy issues.

615
00:30:48,596 --> 00:30:50,138
- Okay, that's enough.
- I'll tell you what.

616
00:30:50,223 --> 00:30:53,475
Your average therapist is gonna charge you
$200-$300 for this sesh.

617
00:30:53,601 --> 00:30:55,435
Me? I'll take a tenner.

618
00:30:56,437 --> 00:30:58,814
Okay, later, if you're feeling magnanimous...

619
00:30:59,607 --> 00:31:01,066
I'll still take a tenner.

620
00:31:01,776 --> 00:31:03,819
You wanna know who sat
in that chair before you?

621
00:31:03,945 --> 00:31:05,987
Mob bosses, murderers, and thieves.

622
00:31:06,239 --> 00:31:07,489
And you know who put them there?

623
00:31:07,740 --> 00:31:09,491
The guy who's sitting in this chair.

624
00:31:09,742 --> 00:31:13,578
So I warn you,
I can maintain my resolve much longer

625
00:31:13,788 --> 00:31:16,498
than you can maintain that phony arrogance.

626
00:31:16,666 --> 00:31:21,503
And the instant that you even show
the slightest crack in that smug facade,

627
00:31:21,671 --> 00:31:22,838
I'll be there.

628
00:31:23,005 --> 00:31:24,589
I will be all over you like...

629
00:31:24,674 --> 00:31:25,841
Like white on rice?

630
00:31:26,175 --> 00:31:27,676
Sorry. That's unfair.

631
00:31:27,844 --> 00:31:30,011
Let me warn you. I want you to follow.

632
00:31:30,263 --> 00:31:31,596
Because no matter
what you think you might know,

633
00:31:31,681 --> 00:31:34,349
we will always be one step, three steps,
seven steps ahead of you.

634
00:31:34,434 --> 00:31:35,559
And just when you think you're catching up,

635
00:31:35,643 --> 00:31:36,977
that's when we'll be right behind you.

636
00:31:37,186 --> 00:31:38,603
And at no time will you be
anywhere other than

637
00:31:38,688 --> 00:31:39,771
exactly where I want you to be.

638
00:31:39,856 --> 00:31:42,941
So come close. Get all over me
because the closer you think you are,

639
00:31:43,025 --> 00:31:44,359
the less you'll actually see.

640
00:31:44,527 --> 00:31:45,986
- I'm gonna nail you...
- (GRUNTS)

641
00:31:49,991 --> 00:31:51,700
Something wrong with that soda, Miss?

642
00:31:53,619 --> 00:31:54,703
(RATTLING)

643
00:31:55,955 --> 00:31:57,038
(CAN OPENS)

644
00:32:00,626 --> 00:32:01,877
Oh! Shit!

645
00:32:02,462 --> 00:32:03,962
First rule of magic.

646
00:32:04,797 --> 00:32:07,048
Always be the smartest guy in the room.

647
00:32:30,072 --> 00:32:31,948
Boss, what are we doing?
We're letting them go?

648
00:32:32,158 --> 00:32:34,075
They all but admitted
they're gonna do it again.

649
00:32:34,285 --> 00:32:36,161
About a half an hour ago,
you didn't give a damn about them.

650
00:32:36,412 --> 00:32:38,163
- What changed?
- I met them.

651
00:32:38,247 --> 00:32:39,247
Hey. Guys.

652
00:32:39,582 --> 00:32:41,750
Hey, I ran a check on the audience.

653
00:32:42,126 --> 00:32:43,251
Most of it was just filler.

654
00:32:43,336 --> 00:32:45,003
People that Tressler
dragged in to pack the room.

655
00:32:45,087 --> 00:32:48,131
But guess who was sitting there in the back,
filming the whole damn thing?

656
00:32:48,257 --> 00:32:49,257
I don't know.

657
00:32:49,342 --> 00:32:50,801
Thaddeus Bradley.

658
00:32:53,262 --> 00:32:55,430
Thaddeus Bradley,
the guy from TV with the DVDs and shows?

659
00:32:55,556 --> 00:32:56,515
No.

660
00:32:56,599 --> 00:32:58,767
Okay, well, he debunks magicians.

661
00:32:59,018 --> 00:33:00,852
He exposes them.
He shows you how to do their tricks.

662
00:33:00,937 --> 00:33:02,020
Get him on the phone.

663
00:33:02,104 --> 00:33:04,981
- I did. You're having lunch with him in 45.
- Good.

664
00:33:05,149 --> 00:33:07,818
WOMAN: Mr. Bradley started as a magician.

665
00:33:07,902 --> 00:33:09,611
- He was actually quite good.
- Really?

666
00:33:09,946 --> 00:33:12,072
- One of the best.
- You don't say.

667
00:33:13,407 --> 00:33:16,284
"The greatest genius
is in seeing through the illusion. "

668
00:33:16,369 --> 00:33:18,787
That's a man who loves his work.

669
00:33:20,248 --> 00:33:22,040
You sure I can't interest you
in a glass of wine?

670
00:33:22,124 --> 00:33:24,960
No, no, no. No, thank you, sir. We're working.

671
00:33:25,419 --> 00:33:26,586
I will take one.

672
00:33:35,137 --> 00:33:37,681
That's incredible. That's amazing.

673
00:33:39,308 --> 00:33:40,475
Red dye, palmed.

674
00:33:40,560 --> 00:33:43,228
Is that how Jesus did it? That's incredible.

675
00:33:43,312 --> 00:33:44,646
I'm sorry. Can I just take a minute here?

676
00:33:44,730 --> 00:33:47,023
You were one of the great magicians.
Why did you stop?

677
00:33:47,650 --> 00:33:50,318
Do you know how many people
went to see magic performed live

678
00:33:50,403 --> 00:33:51,987
in the last half decade?

679
00:33:52,071 --> 00:33:53,321
About 1.6 million.

680
00:33:53,406 --> 00:33:56,741
You know how many DVDs I sold
in the same period?

681
00:33:56,826 --> 00:33:58,118
Five million.

682
00:33:58,202 --> 00:33:59,244
Right, so it's about the money.

683
00:33:59,328 --> 00:34:00,829
Well, the money is only there

684
00:34:00,955 --> 00:34:04,207
because the need to know
is greater than the desire to be fooled.

685
00:34:04,292 --> 00:34:08,587
Like you, I detest those
who prey upon the gullibility of the masses.

686
00:34:08,671 --> 00:34:12,340
With all due respect,
isn't there a cost, though, to this game?

687
00:34:12,884 --> 00:34:13,925
Cost?

688
00:34:14,010 --> 00:34:15,302
I don't know. Careers.

689
00:34:15,386 --> 00:34:16,511
Lives.

690
00:34:16,596 --> 00:34:19,806
I assume you're referring to Lionel Shrike.

691
00:34:20,266 --> 00:34:22,100
I'm sorry, who?

692
00:34:22,184 --> 00:34:23,685
Lionel Shrike.

693
00:34:23,769 --> 00:34:28,064
A middling magician who drowned
in the Hudson River 30 years ago.

694
00:34:28,190 --> 00:34:31,943
On Mr. Bradley's first special,
he revealed all of Shrike's tricks.

695
00:34:32,028 --> 00:34:35,530
The next year,
Shrike tried to stage a comeback.

696
00:34:35,615 --> 00:34:38,950
He was shackled inside a safe,
and dropped into the river.

697
00:34:39,035 --> 00:34:40,160
But he never resurfaced.

698
00:34:40,244 --> 00:34:42,495
You mean he died?

699
00:34:42,580 --> 00:34:44,372
I didn't kill him.

700
00:34:44,457 --> 00:34:48,710
He killed himself trying to do something
he wasn't prepared to handle.

701
00:34:48,794 --> 00:34:51,004
You do realize this is a game?

702
00:34:51,088 --> 00:34:52,881
Believe me, it's not a game.

703
00:34:52,965 --> 00:34:54,132
It is a game.

704
00:34:54,216 --> 00:34:55,967
You're the game. You're being played.

705
00:34:56,052 --> 00:34:59,429
And your pathetic attempts
at fawning to gain my trust...

706
00:34:59,555 --> 00:35:01,306
I mean, come on.

707
00:35:01,390 --> 00:35:02,390
You got me.

708
00:35:02,475 --> 00:35:03,725
If you really want to know

709
00:35:03,809 --> 00:35:07,604
how a bank in Paris was robbed
from a stage in Las Vegas,

710
00:35:07,688 --> 00:35:09,648
my special comes out next month.

711
00:35:09,732 --> 00:35:10,857
Or I could just bring you in

712
00:35:10,942 --> 00:35:13,401
on obstruction charges
and force you to tell me.

713
00:35:13,486 --> 00:35:15,028
Unless, of course,
what you're trying to say to me is that

714
00:35:15,112 --> 00:35:18,198
you actually don't know how they did it.

715
00:35:20,159 --> 00:35:22,327
Showmanship and theatrics.

716
00:35:22,536 --> 00:35:24,663
When a magician waves his hand and says,

717
00:35:24,872 --> 00:35:26,581
"This is where the magic is happening. "

718
00:35:26,666 --> 00:35:29,042
The real trick is happening somewhere else.

719
00:35:29,543 --> 00:35:32,504
Misdirection. A basic concept of magic.

720
00:35:32,713 --> 00:35:35,006
Not interested in the concepts of magic.

721
00:35:35,174 --> 00:35:36,675
I wanna know how they robbed a bank.

722
00:35:36,842 --> 00:35:39,552
You're an idiot if you think
they robbed a bank.

723
00:35:39,804 --> 00:35:41,054
But don't take my word for it.

724
00:35:41,555 --> 00:35:43,223
How about a trip to Paris?

725
00:35:43,683 --> 00:35:46,726
Hermia, the teleportation helmet,
please, darling?

726
00:35:47,353 --> 00:35:48,687
You're joking, right?

727
00:35:48,854 --> 00:35:50,689
You wanna know how they did it or not?

728
00:35:51,857 --> 00:35:53,858
I'm just gonna put this on your head.

729
00:35:55,236 --> 00:35:56,569
THADDEUS: Step right up.

730
00:35:57,822 --> 00:35:59,155
There you go.

731
00:36:00,199 --> 00:36:03,827
Now we just pull this down all safe and snug.

732
00:36:04,870 --> 00:36:07,539
Make yourself comfortable. There we go.

733
00:36:07,999 --> 00:36:12,252
And when you're ready to go to Paris,
just say the magic word.

734
00:36:12,420 --> 00:36:13,420
Blow me?

735
00:36:14,672 --> 00:36:16,423
- That'll suffice.
- Bon voyage.

736
00:36:19,760 --> 00:36:20,927
(SCREAMING)

737
00:36:27,393 --> 00:36:28,685
THADDEUS: Bonjour.

738
00:36:28,769 --> 00:36:31,896
I personally prefer to take the stairs to Paris.

739
00:36:32,398 --> 00:36:34,315
Welcome to the City of Light, Agent Rhodes.

740
00:36:34,400 --> 00:36:36,151
Hardy har har. What was with the helmet?

741
00:36:36,819 --> 00:36:37,902
My fun.

742
00:36:38,863 --> 00:36:40,280
- (LAUGHS)
- What?

743
00:36:40,740 --> 00:36:41,823
- Nothing.
- Oh!

744
00:36:43,743 --> 00:36:45,493
- So they got a vault set.
- Yes.

745
00:36:45,578 --> 00:36:48,413
And it's the exact replica
of the little French guy's bank.

746
00:36:48,497 --> 00:36:51,041
- So he's a plant.
- He was a dupe, not a plant.

747
00:36:51,125 --> 00:36:53,501
They chose him.
They knew which seat he was in.

748
00:36:53,586 --> 00:36:55,545
No, no, no, the audience picked the seat.

749
00:36:56,922 --> 00:36:59,507
Unless, of course,
the audience didn't pick the seat.

750
00:36:59,592 --> 00:37:01,634
They let them think they picked the seat.

751
00:37:01,927 --> 00:37:03,344
We'll choose one at random then.

752
00:37:03,429 --> 00:37:05,472
THADDEUS: Actually, they were
just palming the ball

753
00:37:05,556 --> 00:37:07,265
with the seat number they wanted.

754
00:37:07,975 --> 00:37:09,809
DANIEL: We are looking at row number 5.

755
00:37:09,894 --> 00:37:11,227
Henley, could I please have a seat number?

756
00:37:11,604 --> 00:37:12,562
